WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.280
Hey, welcome back.

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:06.540
Last time, a magnetic field bent the path of a moving charge.

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:12.000
Today, many moving charges are inside a conducting wire, and their magnetic

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:13.680
forces are transferred to the conductor.

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:16.000
The wire can bend or move.

00:00:16.920 --> 00:00:21.300
For a tiny current element, the force is I times d l cross the external

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:22.100
magnetic field.

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:25.600
The vector d l points along conventional current.

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:30.564
In a metal, electrons drift the opposite way, but we do not reverse the

00:00:30.564 --> 00:00:30.980
current

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:32.440
arrow again in this formula.

00:00:33.860 --> 00:00:37.300
The negative charge and opposite drift direction are already accounted for.

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:42.260
For a straight segment in a uniform field, add all the elements to get I times

00:00:42.260 --> 00:00:43.560
the length vector cross B.

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:48.719
Its magnitude is I L B sine theta, where theta is between current direction

00:00:48.719 --> 00:00:49.300
and field.

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:52.260
Picture current to the right with B into the page.

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:55.540
Right cross into the page gives upward force on the wire.

00:00:56.660 --> 00:00:58.680
Reverse only the current and force becomes downward.

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:02.240
Reverse only the field and it also becomes downward.

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:05.320
Reverse both and the force returns upward.

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:09.220
A wire parallel to B has zero magnetic force in this model.

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:13.960
Always identify the particular wire and the field produced by other sources.

00:01:14.980 --> 00:01:18.920
We are not inserting the wire's own field into a formula for its net sideways

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:19.300
force.

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:24.600
Draw current, field and force as three separate directions before reaching for

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:24.840
numbers.

00:01:25.152 --> 00:01:28.472
Let's calculate a force before building a more complicated circuit.

00:01:29.612 --> 00:01:33.607
A straight forty centimeter segment carries three amperes in a uniform field

00:01:33.607 --> 00:01:33.712
of

00:01:33.712 --> 00:01:35.132
zero point two zero tesla.

00:01:36.252 --> 00:01:37.412
Current and field are perpendicular.

00:01:38.652 --> 00:01:41.052
Convert forty centimeters to zero point four zero meters.

00:01:42.092 --> 00:01:44.832
Multiply three by zero point four by zero point two.

00:01:45.832 --> 00:01:47.772
The force is zero point two four newtons.

00:01:48.912 --> 00:01:51.752
Now let the angle between current and field be thirty degrees.

00:01:52.552 --> 00:01:56.632
Sine thirty is one half, so the force becomes zero point one two newtons.

00:01:57.892 --> 00:02:02.812
Be careful if a diagram labels the complementary angle: first find the actual

00:02:02.812 --> 00:02:03.992
angle between current and B.

00:02:04.852 --> 00:02:07.572
Only the length inside the field belongs in the simple expression.

00:02:09.132 --> 00:02:12.532
Suppose a horizontal wire has mass twelve grams and length thirty centimeters

00:02:12.672 --> 00:02:15.792
in a perpendicular field of zero point four zero tesla.

00:02:17.092 --> 00:02:20.672
Assume negligible forces from connecting leads and use gravitational

00:02:20.672 --> 00:02:22.972
acceleration ten meters per second squared.

00:02:24.432 --> 00:02:25.972
Weight is zero point one two newtons.

00:02:27.172 --> 00:02:31.972
For magnetic force to support the wire, set I L B equal to m g.

00:02:31.972 --> 00:02:34.072
The required current magnitude is one ampere.

00:02:34.492 --> 00:02:35.252
Is that the whole answer?

00:02:36.412 --> 00:02:37.472
Pause and check the arrows.

00:02:38.672 --> 00:02:42.492
With field into the page, current must be rightward to produce upward support.

00:02:43.272 --> 00:02:46.872
A leftward current of the same magnitude would add a downward magnetic force

00:02:46.872 --> 00:02:47.252
instead.

00:02:47.252 --> 00:02:50.620
A correct number paired with the wrong direction does not describe

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:51.232
equilibrium.

00:02:51.552 --> 00:02:54.412
Does a bent wire require a new force law?

00:02:55.012 --> 00:02:55.292
No.

00:02:56.172 --> 00:02:58.572
Add I d l cross B along its path.

00:02:59.272 --> 00:03:02.743
If current is steady and the external field is uniform everywhere on the

00:03:02.743 --> 00:03:03.032
chosen

00:03:03.032 --> 00:03:06.092
wire, I and B can be taken outside the integral.

00:03:06.692 --> 00:03:10.412
The sum of the tiny displacement vectors is the vector from starting endpoint

00:03:10.412 --> 00:03:13.412
to finishing endpoint, following conventional current.

00:03:14.072 --> 00:03:18.192
So the total force is I times that endpoint displacement cross B.

00:03:18.712 --> 00:03:21.652
It is not I times the curved path length times B.

00:03:22.552 --> 00:03:25.252
The turns in the path make some vector contributions cancel.

00:03:26.552 --> 00:03:31.092
Consider an upper semicircular wire of radius ten centimeters, carrying three

00:03:31.092 --> 00:03:33.772
amperes from its left endpoint to its right endpoint.

00:03:34.692 --> 00:03:39.412
The entire arc is in a uniform field of zero point two zero tesla into the

00:03:39.412 --> 00:03:39.692
page.

00:03:40.852 --> 00:03:43.392
Endpoint displacement is twenty centimeters to the right.

00:03:44.232 --> 00:03:47.252
The net force is therefore zero point one two newtons upward.

00:03:48.732 --> 00:03:51.972
Using the semicircle's arc length would incorrectly add magnitudes without

00:03:51.972 --> 00:03:53.092
respecting their directions.

00:03:54.192 --> 00:03:58.212
If a complete closed loop lies in the same uniform field, starting and ending

00:03:58.212 --> 00:03:59.092
points coincide.

00:03:59.912 --> 00:04:01.412
Its net magnetic force is zero.

00:04:02.332 --> 00:04:06.212
That does not mean every piece feels zero force, and it does not rule out a

00:04:06.212 --> 00:04:07.052
torque that turns the loop.

00:04:07.852 --> 00:04:09.452
We will treat torque in the next class.

00:04:10.012 --> 00:04:13.672
If the field varies along the wire, or only part of it lies inside the field,

00:04:14.232 --> 00:04:17.232
apply the integral or sum to the actual field covered pieces.

00:04:18.172 --> 00:04:20.332
Do not extend this shortcut beyond its assumptions.

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:23.820
Now let two wires provide each other's magnetic field.

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:28.944
Assume long, straight, thin parallel wires in vacuum or approximately air,

00:04:28.944 --> 00:04:29.180
with

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:31.040
steady currents and negligible end effects.

00:04:31.820 --> 00:04:33.720
Let their axis separation be d.

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:39.680
Wire one produces a field of magnitude mu zero I one divided by two pi d at

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:40.200
wire two.

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:43.060
This field is perpendicular to the second wire.

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:48.340
Multiply by I two and the selected length L to find the force on that length.

00:04:48.340 --> 00:04:52.940
Force per unit length is mu zero times the product of the current magnitudes

00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:55.320
divided by two pi d.

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:55.920
Where does it point?

00:04:56.520 --> 00:04:59.120
Draw both currents upward, with wire one on the left.

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:01.960
Its field at the right wire is into the page.

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:06.380
Upward current cross into page field points left, toward wire one.

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:08.300
The left wire is pushed right.

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:10.300
Same direction currents attract.

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:14.980
Reverse one current and both forces point away from the other wire: opposite

00:05:14.980 --> 00:05:15.660
currents repel.

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:19.940
Use five amperes and eight amperes separated by four centimeters.

00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:25.400
Taking mu zero approximately four pi times ten to the minus seven gives force

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:28.780
per meter two times ten to the minus four newtons per meter.

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:33.731
On a twenty centimeter portion far from the ends, that is four times ten to

00:05:33.731 --> 00:05:33.900
the

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:34.800
minus five newtons.

00:05:35.740 --> 00:05:39.451
Doubling either current doubles the force per meter; doubling separation

00:05:39.451 --> 00:05:39.760
halves

00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:39.980
it.

00:05:40.500 --> 00:05:44.520
The wire formula uses axis separation, not the gap between surfaces.

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:47.880
Keep the direction check separate from the magnitude calculation.

00:05:48.216 --> 00:05:52.376
Let's finish with a three wire problem that rewards a careful sketch.

00:05:53.336 --> 00:05:58.296
Two long fixed parallel wires carry four amperes and six amperes upward,

00:05:59.236 --> 00:06:00.536
separated by twenty five centimeters.

00:06:01.296 --> 00:06:04.576
Put a third parallel wire between them carrying two amperes upward.

00:06:05.316 --> 00:06:07.296
Both outer wires attract the middle wire.

00:06:08.056 --> 00:06:10.536
Let x be its distance from the four ampere wire.

00:06:11.176 --> 00:06:14.416
Its distance from the six ampere wire is d minus x.

00:06:14.716 --> 00:06:18.656
For zero sideways net force, the two force magnitudes must match.

00:06:19.356 --> 00:06:23.853
The common factor mu zero I three divided by two pi cancels, leaving I one

00:06:23.853 --> 00:06:24.096
over

00:06:24.096 --> 00:06:26.456
x equals I two over d minus x.

00:06:27.436 --> 00:06:31.236
Solve to get x equal to I one d divided by I one plus I two.

00:06:31.736 --> 00:06:35.756
Here x is ten centimeters, so the other distance is fifteen centimeters.

00:06:36.516 --> 00:06:40.134
Each attraction is one point six times ten to the minus five newtons per

00:06:40.134 --> 00:06:40.436
meter.

00:06:41.156 --> 00:06:44.896
The balance point is nearer the smaller current, because a smaller source

00:06:44.896 --> 00:06:47.396
current needs a shorter distance to give the same field.

00:06:48.456 --> 00:06:51.796
Now nudge the middle wire slightly right while the outer wires and currents

00:06:51.796 --> 00:06:52.396
stay fixed.

00:06:52.916 --> 00:06:55.816
The rightward attraction grows and the leftward attraction weakens.

00:06:56.516 --> 00:07:00.916
The wire is pushed farther right: this lateral equilibrium is unstable for our

00:07:00.916 --> 00:07:02.116
same direction middle current.

00:07:02.896 --> 00:07:05.196
Force balance and stability are different questions.

00:07:05.996 --> 00:07:10.016
Reversing the middle current reverses both pairwise forces without moving the

00:07:10.016 --> 00:07:10.876
zero force position.

00:07:11.976 --> 00:07:15.736
Finally, if the two outer currents run in opposite directions, their fields

00:07:15.736 --> 00:07:18.076
between the wires reinforce rather than cancel.

00:07:18.996 --> 00:07:22.835
There is no intervening zero field balance point for a nonzero third current

00:07:22.835 --> 00:07:22.936
in

00:07:22.936 --> 00:07:23.656
this ideal model.
